<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/expert/quantum-field-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Quantum field theory (QFT) merges quantum mechanics with special relativity — and in doing so
    it <strong>demotes particles from protagonists to bit-players</strong>. The fundamental
    objects are fields filling all space; particles are their quantised ripples. Two electrons
    anywhere in the universe are identical because they are the <em>same</em> field, plucked
    twice. This reframing is what makes particle creation, annihilation and the forces
    comprehensible.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the conceptual ladder of QFT, from fields to the Standard Model" widget="equationStep" config={smCfg} />

  <h2>Why QFT is physics' most precise theory</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The electron's magnetic moment</strong> is predicted by quantum electrodynamics (QED) to 12 significant figures — the best theory-experiment agreement in science. Feynman, Schwinger and Tomonaga won the 1965 Nobel for taming its infinities.</li>
    <li><strong>Renormalisation:</strong> early QFT exploded with infinities; the fix (absorbing them into measured masses and charges) looked like sweeping dirt under rugs — but the rugs match every experiment, and Wilson later showed why (1974, Nobel 1982).</li>
    <li><strong>The Higgs mechanism (1964, confirmed 2012):</strong> a field that fills space and drags on particles, giving them mass. The Large Hadron Collider found its ripple at 125 GeV — the last piece of the Standard Model.</li>
    <li><strong>What's outside:</strong> gravity has no working quantum field theory (the century's open problem); neutrino masses and dark matter already hint the inventory is incomplete.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Feynman, QED (1985); Peskin & Schroeder, An Introduction to Quantum Field Theory; ATLAS & CMS, Phys. Lett. B (2012).</p>
</TopicPage>
